/** @page scheduler Outgoing send scheduler

ENet's outgoing queues can be configured per peer to protect time-critical
state updates from reliable-event backlog.

@section scheduler_model Scheduling model

The scheduler uses the existing ENet outgoing command queues:

- Reliable data is placed in the reliable queue and is never discarded.
- Unreliable data is placed in the unreliable queue and is evicted by
  priority when the queue reaches its byte or command limit.
- Both queues are selected with a deterministic weighted command budget.
  The default is 1 reliable command for every 4 unreliable commands.
- A full reliable queue returns @ref ENET_SEND_RESULT_BACKPRESSURE
  immediately. The caller keeps ownership of the packet and can retry later.
  No network I/O or waiting is performed by enet_peer_send_ex().
- A full unreliable queue evicts older packets with priority lower than or
  equal to the incoming packet. If the incoming packet cannot displace
  anything, it is dropped and @ref ENET_SEND_RESULT_DROPPED is returned.
- ENET_SEND_PRIORITY_HIGH also bypasses ENet's adaptive unreliable throttle.
  It can still be lost by the network, but not by local congestion throttling.

@section scheduler_channels Channels

Place reliable events and position updates on separate ENet channels. ENet
associates unreliable commands with the last reliable sequence number on the
same channel. If both classes share a channel, a missing reliable event can
hold later unreliable commands at the receiver until the reliable retransmit
arrives, regardless of sender-side scheduling.

@section scheduler_api API

Configure a peer after enet_host_connect() or after accepting a connection:

@code
ENetSendSchedulerConfig config = {
   1, 4,
   256 * 1024, 32 * 1024,
   1024, 256
};
enet_peer_send_scheduler_configure(peer, &config);
@endcode

Queue a position update and a reliable event:

@code
int positionResult = enet_peer_send_ex(peer, POSITION_CHANNEL, positionPacket,
                                       ENET_SEND_PRIORITY_HIGH);
int eventResult = enet_peer_send_ex(peer, EVENT_CHANNEL, eventPacket,
                                    ENET_SEND_PRIORITY_NORMAL);

if (eventResult == ENET_SEND_RESULT_BACKPRESSURE) {
   /* Keep eventPacket and retry after the host has been serviced. */
}
@endcode

On any result other than ENET_SEND_RESULT_OK, the caller still owns the
packet and must destroy it or retry it. On success, ENet owns the packet.

Queue and eviction counters are available through
enet_peer_send_scheduler_get_stats().

@section scheduler_loss High packet loss

ENet's default disconnect policy can be too aggressive for a 30% packet loss
link because it disconnects after a short retry budget. For deployments that
must deliver reliable events over such links, raise the retry budget:

@code
enet_peer_timeout(peer, 1024, 30000, 120000);
@endcode

The scheduler itself never drops reliable data. The transport must be allowed
to retransmit long enough for the required delivery guarantee.

@section scheduler_benchmark Benchmark

Build and run the loopback benchmark:

@code
cmake -S . -B build -DENET_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON
cmake --build build
./build/enet_scheduler_benchmark 0
./build/enet_scheduler_benchmark 30
@endcode

The benchmark creates 128 real ENet connections, fills each reliable queue
beyond its configured limit, fills the unreliable queue with low-priority
noise, sends high-priority position updates at 60 Hz, and drops 30% of UDP
datagrams in both directions after connection setup.
*/
